   "text": "The main phase is **Sr2HfCrO6 (mp-1095039)**; the second phase is likely **KSr(BiO3)2 (mp-1223282)**, but that call is tentative.\n\n**Main phase.** Most observed peaks index to a cubic, rock-salt-ordered double perovskite with a \u2248 7.88 \u00c5:\n\n| Observed 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) | d (\u00c5) | Index | Intensity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 19.45 | 4.56 | (111) superlattice | 35 |\n| 22.65 | 3.92 | (200) | 17 |\n| 32.0 | 2.795 | (220) | 100 |\n| 37.85 | 2.37 | (311) | 16 |\n| 46.2 | 1.96 | (400) | 34 |\n| 50.65 | 1.80 | (331) | 12 |\n| 57.25 | 1.61 | (422) | 31 |\n\n- The strong (111) superlattice peak points to a large contrast between the two ordered B-site cations. Hf and Cr fit that.\n- Sr2HfCrO6 matches this pattern with a lattice about 1.5% smaller than its CIF value (8.0 \u00c5).\n- LaRuO3 has the same cell volume but is a strongly distorted, unordered Pnma perovskite. It would not give this clean cubic pattern with a strong (111).\n\n**Second phase.** One extra broad peak at 29.2\u201329.7\u00b0 (d \u2248 3.04 \u00c5, intensity about 24) is not indexable on the cubic cell.\n\n- KSr(BiO3)2 fits best. Its I4/m cell (a \u2248 5.99 \u00c5, c \u2248 8.72 \u00c5) places the split (112)/(200) pair at about 29.4\u00b0 and 29.8\u00b0. Its weaker reflections would sit near the noise level.\n- Ba2CeZrO6 (220) is the alternative, at 28.9\u00b0 unstrained. It would only match if its lattice were contracted by about 1.2%.\n- I could not separate these two from secondary peaks, because both predict their next reflections at almost the same angles (about 41.9\u00b0 and 51.9\u00b0), where there is no clear signal above noise.\n- A shoulder at about 56.9\u00b0 (d \u2248 1.617 \u00c5) is not explained by either candidate.\n\nThe remaining candidates are ruled out by missing strong lines:\n- Li4TiFe3O8 (spinel) would show its strongest peak near 35.1\u00b0.\n- SmCuO2 (delafossite) would show a strong (104) near 35\u00b0.\n- NaNiO2 would show a strong layer peak near 16.6\u00b0.\n- SrLaCoO4 has no strong line near 29.3\u00b0.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1095039\", \"mp-1223282\"], \"confidence\": 0.55, \"note\": \"Ordered double perovskite Sr2HfCrO6 (a~7.88 \u00c5, strong (111) superlattice at 19.45\u00b0) dominates; the extra broad peak near 29.3\u00b0 is tentatively assigned to KSr(BiO3)2, though Ba2CeZrO6 cannot be excluded.\"}",
